"Humans Need Not Apply (https://yalebooks.yale.edu/book/9780300223576/humans-need-not-apply/)\nciteturn136search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; Image: Cover of 'Humans Need Not Apply' by Jerry Kaplan ... Selected as one of the 10 best science and technology books of 2015 by The Economist ... As society stands on the cusp of unprecedented change, Jerry Kaplan unpacks the latest advances in robotics, machine learning, and perception powering systems that rival or exceed human capabilities.\n\nImage: Cover of 'Humans Need Not Apply' by Jerry Kaplan \n\nAlso Available:\n\n# Humans Need Not Apply\n\n## A Guide to Wealth and Work in the Age of Artificial Intelligence\n\nby Jerry Kaplan\n\n256 Pages, 5.50 × 8.25 in\n\n  * Paperback\n  * 9780300223576\n  * Published: Tuesday, 22 Nov 2016\n\n$20.00\n\nBUY\n\nAlso Available At:\n\n  * Amazon\n  * Barnes & Noble\n  * Bookshop\n  * Indiebound\n  * Indigo\n  * Powell's\n  * Seminary Co-op\n\n  * Description\n  * Authors\n  * Praise\n\nAn insightful, engaging tour by a noted Silicon Valley insider of how accelerating developments in Artificial Intelligence will transform the way we live and work\nSelected as one of the 10 best science and technology books of 2015 by The Economist\n \nAfter billions of dollars and fifty years of effort, researchers are finally cracking the code on artificial intelligence. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRobots in Our Midst: A Conversation with Jerry Kaplan - Yale University Press (https://yalebooks.yale.edu/2015/07/29/robots-in-our-midst-a-conversation-with-jerry-kaplan-2/)\nciteturn136search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; July 29, 2015 | yalepress | TechnologyJerry Kaplan, author of Humans Need Not Apply, says the robots are coming, but whether they will be working on behalf of society or a small cadre of the super-rich is very much in doubt. ... The new arms race is already on, and we need to be prepared not only to defeat systems developed by others, but also to safely and humanely control those that we deploy to defend us.\n\n# Robots in Our Midst: A Conversation with Jerry Kaplan\n\nJuly 29, 2015 | yalepress | Technology\n\nJerry Kaplan, author of Humans Need Not Apply, says the robots are coming, but whether they will be working on behalf of society or a small cadre of the super-rich is very much in doubt. We sat down with him to discuss what the future may look like and how humans might fit into this new reality.\n\nYale University Press: So is the future going to be more like Star Trek or Terminator?\n\nJerry Kaplan: That depends on who you are. Advances percolating in research labs around the world are poised to have a far greater impact on our lives and livelihoods than most people realize. While these new technologies will be dazzling—self-driving cars, robotic farmers, computerized drug discovery—our social and economic systems may not be up to the task of distributing the benefits broadly across society. As people lose their jobs while the profits accrue to the already wealthy, we may experience a protracted period of social turmoil and extreme income inequality. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHumans Need Not Apply: A Guide to Wealth & Work in the Age of Artificial ... - Jerry Kaplan - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books/about/Humans_Need_Not_Apply.html?id=yatJCgAAQBAJ)\nciteturn136search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Humans Need Not Apply: A Guide to Wealth & Work in the Age of Artificial IntelligenceJerry Kaplan Yale University Press, Aug 4, 2015 - Technology & Engineering - 258 pages An \"intriguing, insightful\" look at how algorithms and robots could lead to social unrest—and how to avoid it ( The Economist, Books of the Year). ... Society stands on the cusp of unprecedented change, driven by advances in robotics, machine le